    },
    {
      "name": "IntergovernmentalOrganization",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/gist/IntergovernmentalOrganization",
      "description": "An organization whose members are government organizations. This can comprise regional, municipal, state/province, or national level entities.",
      "notes": [
        "OWL equivalentClass: (gist:Organization & \u22652^isMemberOf.gist:GovernmentOrganization)"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "value": "The United Nations, the European Union, the Metropolitan Transit Authority.",
          "@type": "Example"
        }
      ],
      "in_subset": [
        "gist_core"
      ],
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/gist/core",
      "aliases": [
        "Intergovernmental Organization"
      ],
      "mappings": [
        "gist:IntergovernmentalOrganization"
      ],
      "is_a": "Organization",
      "slots": [
        "name",
        "description"
      ],
      "slot_usage": {},
      "class_uri": "https://w3id.org/semanticarts/ns/ontology/gist/IntergovernmentalOrganization",
      "@type": "ClassDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "KnowledgeConcept",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/gist/KnowledgeConcept",
      "description": "An abstract concept that arises from the distillation of experience. It is similar to a category but, rather than being a simple tag, it has rich structure.",
      "comments": [
        "Some knowledge is about specific instances that already exist in the knowledge graph. We may have knowledge that Judge Jones is more lenient on repeat offenders in the morning; we may know that the earliest killing frost in Fort Collins is the last week of September.\r\n\r\nThese fit the broader definition of knowledge, the distillation of experience. But they do not require any new instances. Judge Jones and Fort Collins already exist and have a place in our knowledge graph.\r\n\r\nBut some knowledge requires that we synthesize new instances in order to have a place to consolidate the knowledge. A disease isn't a tangible thing, like a person or a building. A disease is a prediction that a person's health will decline in a predictable way (without treatment and with treatment). The number of diseases continues to grow as we collectively learn more and more granular distinctions. Lung cancer used to be a single disease, but now we have dozens of fine-grained distinctions; e.g., non-lymphoma small cell fusiform is different from alveolar adenocarcinoma because we now know the prognosis and treatment are different.\r\n\r\nThis superclass is meant as a place for subclasses that will have the instances that represent the foci of the knowledge we have acquired. Note the distinction between a particular portion of, say, gold, which instantiates gist:PhysicalSubstance, and the concept of gold, which instantiates gist:KnowledgeConcept.\r\n\r\nIn some ontologies what we are calling knowledge concepts are defined as classes; e.g., non-lymphoma small cell fusiform and alveolar adenocarcinoma would be two classes rather than two instances. But using a class makes it harder to connect the concept to other instances in a knowledge graph, and furthermore such classes would lack instances."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "value": "Most domains will define a few subclasses, such as gene, protein, chemical, disease, subject matter, industry, method.",
          "@type": "Example"
        }
      ],
      "in_subset": [
        "gist_core"
      ],
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/gist/core",
      "aliases": [
        "Knowledge Concept"
      ],
      "mappings": [
        "gist:KnowledgeConcept"
      ],
      "related_mappings": [
        "iso22989:KnowledgeGraph"
      ],
      "is_a": "IntellectualProperty",
      "slots": [
        "name",
        "description"
      ],
      "slot_usage": {},
      "class_uri": "https://w3id.org/semanticarts/ns/ontology/gist/KnowledgeConcept",
      "@type": "ClassDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "Landmark",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/gist/Landmark",
      "description": "Something permanently attached to the Earth.",
      "deprecated": "true",
      "notes": [
        "OWL subClassOf restrictions: \u2203hasPhysicalLocation.(gist:GeoRegion | gist:GeoVolume)",
        "See guidance on removing the term in the next major release at https://github.com/semanticarts/gist/issues/947#issuecomment-1679566885."
      ],
      "in_subset": [
        "gist_core"
      ],
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/gist/core",
      "aliases": [
        "Landmark"
      ],
      "mappings": [
        "gist:Landmark"
      ],
      "is_a": "PhysicalIdentifiableItem",
      "slots": [
        "name",
        "description"
      ],
      "slot_usage": {},
      "class_uri": "https://w3id.org/semanticarts/ns/ontology/gist/Landmark",
      "@type": "ClassDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "Language",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/gist/Language",
      "description": "A recognized, organized set of symbols and grammar.",
      "deprecated": "true",
      "examples": [
        {
          "value": "Natural languages such as English and Spanish; computer languages such as OWL, Python, and XML.",
          "@type": "Example"
        }
      ],
      "in_subset": [
        "gist_core"
